Introduction
Plastics are widely utilized due to their cost-effectiveness, versatility, and durability (Andrady and Neal, 2009; Harrison and Hester, 2018). Global plastic production was recorded at 335 million tons in 2016, increasing to 391 million tons by 2021 (Plastics Europe, 2022). According to Addor et al. (2022), it is projected that by 2050, annual global plastic production will reach 29.2 billion tons, with annual plastic waste up to 24.52 million tons. In 2015, only 9 % of the plastic waste was generated globally was recycled, while 12 % was incinerated, and 79 % was either deposited in landfills or released into the natural environment (Geyer et al., 2017). The annual amount of plastic waste entering terrestrial ecosystems is estimated to exceed that of the oceans by a factor ranging from 4 to 23 (Horton et al., 2017).
Microplastics (MPs), defined as particles with a diameter smaller than 5 mm, were first characterized by Thompson et al. (2004). They are classified into two categories: primary and secondary MPs (Cole et al., 2011). Primary MPs are directly produced with a dimension <5 mm in intentionally or unintentionally manufacturing processes. Examples of primary MPs include plastic microbeads found in detergents and cosmetics, as well as particles released during the production and processing of plastics (Sun et al., 2020a). In contrast, secondary MPs are formed as a result of the degradation of plastic materials in the environment, impacted by physical, chemical, and biological factors (Browne et al., 2007). Initially, research on MP pollution predominantly addressed marine ecosystems. However, recent studies broadened this focus to include terrestrial environments (Rillig, 2012), with observations of soil MPs emerging globally (Auta et al., 2017; Dong et al., 2021a; Leitão et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2021d; Wang et al., 2022b; Zhang et al., 2022b). The abundance of MPs varies significantly across studies, with discrepancies of up to eight orders of magnitude (Koutnik et al., 2021). Key sources of soil MPs include plastic mulching, organic or sludge fertilization, wastewater irrigation, surface runoff, and atmospheric deposition, with plastic mulching identified as the predominant contributor in agricultural soils (Yang et al., 2022).
MPs are characterized by their small particle sizes, potential toxicity, prolonged persistence in the environment, and ease of absorption by living organisms (Luo et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2022d). During the second United Nations Environment Assembly convened in 2016, MPs pollution was identified as the second most significant scientific issue challenge within the domain of environmental and ecological science research (Tang et al., 2023). MPs can enter the human body through various pathways, including respiration (Gasperi et al., 2018), dermal absorption (Bhat et al., 2023), and the food chain (Prata and Dias-Pereira, 2023). Recent studies have consistently found MPs in human lungs, blood, and feces (Barceló et al., 2023; Jung et al., 2022; Pérez-Guevara et al., 2021). The potential adverse impacts of MPs on human health include oxidative stress responses, cellular damage, tissue inflammation, and chemical toxicity (He et al., 2023a; Prokić et al., 2019).
MPs significantly impact the physical and chemical properties of soil, including water retention, bulk density, and pH (Li et al., 2023). Through various physical, chemical, and biological processes, MPs can undergo aging and fragmentation within soil matrices. Additionally, MPs can serve as vectors for heavy metals, organic pollutants, pathogens, and antibiotics (Ren et al., 2021), with their adsorption capacity increasing in relation to specific surface area during as they age. Therefore, the aging and fragmentation of MPs may exacerbate the potential risks associated with these contaminants in the environment. Furthermore, chemical additives present in plastics (e.g., pigments, plasticizers, stabilizers, flame retardants, and antioxidants) may leach from aging MPs, thereby introducing potentially hazardous substances into the surrounding ecosystem (Huang et al., 2021; Sheng et al., 2021). MPs can be transported into deeper soil layers through soil pore leaching, plant growth, animal ingestion, agricultural practices, and other biological disturbances. Previous studies have identified concentrations of 15 items/L (Panno et al., 2019) and 54 items/L (Balestra et al., 2023) in groundwater in the United States and Italy, respectively, providing evidence that MPs can indeed be transported from soil to groundwater.
Despite the extensive research conducted on MPs in aquatic systems, there remains a significant gap in the literature regarding MPs in soil environment (Liu et al., 2024). Consequently, this article focuses on three primary areas of investigation: (1) Systematically identifying the emission sources of MPs in soil. A comprehensive understanding of the pathways through which MPs infiltrate soil is essential for developing effective management strategies aimed at mitigating or preventing MP pollution; (2) Examining the distribution, abundance and characteristics of MPs under different land uses from a global perspective, and comparatively analyzing the different quantitative indicators of MP abundance. This analysis serves as a reference for future research concerning the ecological risks of associated with MPs; and (3) Understanding the diversity of soil MPs transport behaviors, examining the internal influencing mechanisms and the external mechanisms driving their movement, which is crucial for predicting potential threats to soil ecosystems.
